<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Finland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> is the easternmost arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, lying between Finland to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> to the south, with St Petersburg at its eastern end. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it carried the trade between the Hanseatic towns and Novgorod via the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>, and Reval (Tallinn) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a> grew on its shores. Control of its coasts was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the Livonian powers for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland.jpg" alt="Gulf of Finland" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NormanEinstein: NormanEinstein; derivative work by Materialscientist · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Sea_map2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> covers 30,000 km2 and runs about 400 km from the Hanko Peninsula to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. It is shallow, averaging 38 m deep, and its water has very low salinity because of the rivers that flow into it, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>. Parts of it can freeze from late November to late April. The northern coast is high and winding, with many skerries, while the southern shore is smooth and shallow and runs along a limestone escarpment, the Baltic Klint, up to 55 m high.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People settled the shores soon after the glaciers retreated, and sites up to 9,000 years old have been found. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> later settled the coast: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, Votes, Izhorians and the Korela. From the 8th to the 13th century the gulf and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> formed part of the waterway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and from the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a> controlled the eastern coast. Swedes conquered the Finnish tribes to the north in the 12th and 13th centuries, and in 1293 Torkel Knutsson founded Vyborg Castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-noteborg">Treaty of Nöteborg</a> of 1323 set the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Reval, now Tallinn, was established in the 13th century, and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> of 1219.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> reclaimed the eastern part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> (1700–1721). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> was founded at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> on 16 May 1703, and the fortresses of Kronstadt, nicknamed the Russian Dardanelles, were built to protect it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a> in 1710, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 gave Russia the lands along the Neva and the gulf, as well as Estland and Swedish Livonia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fredrikshamn">Treaty of Fredrikshamn</a> after the war of 1808–1809 gave Russia Finland, which became a Grand Duchy. Finland declared independence on 6 December 1917 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The bottom of the gulf is one of the largest ship cemeteries in the world, since cold, low-salinity water and the absence of shipworms preserve wrecks well. In 1721 more than 100 vessels were lost within three months during the evacuation of Russian troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Helsinki and Tallinn both face the gulf, as does <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> at its eastern end.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Finland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grain from the estates of Harria and Vironia was another export, and the town also traded with Finland and Sweden across the <b>Gulf of Finland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The clash was part of the Swedish–Novgorodian Wars over the <b>Gulf of Finland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-15">15 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
